        <body>
            <div xml:id="d712">

                <pb facs="0940" n="826" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg826"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.50 Am 3/25</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Secretary of
                        Commerce</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hoover</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-07-15"><hi rendition="#italic">July 15,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I have received your note
                    of July fourteenth and I am in accord.</p>
                <p>I have been considering, and I am inclined to favor, the sending of a technical
                    expert mission to study the economic situation in Russia. The preliminary steps
                    are important and I shall take these under advisement.</p>
                <p>In view of the failure of the Powers to accomplish anything of great importance
                    at Genoa and The Hague, and on the assumption that an international expert
                    commission would not be permitted to conduct such an investigation in Russia,
                    the opportunity seems to be ours and we should take such action as would dispel
                    the notion that we are indifferent, and, on the other hand, should encourage the
                    view that we are proceeding carefully to find out the facts and shape our policy
                    in accordance with them. This has always been the view I have entertained.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
